Council","xy":[-1.2536,52.88927]},"19858":{"h":10,"osm":"way/149967596","kind":"agricultural","council":"Erewash Borough Council","xy":[-1.25313,52.88868]},"19859":{"h":10,"osm":"way/149967600","kind":"agricultural","council":"Erewash Borough Council","xy":[-1.2532,52.88909]},"19860":{"h":10,"osm":"way/149967597","kind":"agricultural","council":"Erewash Borough Council","xy":[-1.25261,52.88921]},"19861":{"h":10,"osm":"way/275946691","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.24722,52.87663]},"19862":{"y":1612,"f":"approx","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1607–17","h":8,"osm":"way/55087219","kind":"residential","listEntry":1242464,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1242464","qid":"Q7798580","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Thrumpton%20Hall%2C%20Nottinghamshire%20%28geograph%201061761%29.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrumpton_Hall","cat":"Thrumpton Hall","stories":[{"layer":"thrumpton","text":"George Fitzroy Seymour owned the estate from 1949 to 1994, and Miranda Seymour has owned it since.","source":{"title":"Thrumpton Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrumpton_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Owners since 1949"},{"layer":"thrumpton","text":"The hall came to the Byrons when George Byron, 8th Baron Byron, married Lucy Wescomb in 1843, and they kept it for about a hundred years. Frederick, the 10th Baron, was the village's vicar and from 1917 its squire as well: its \"squarson\".","source":{"title":"Thrumpton Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrumpton_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"The Byrons of Thrumpton"},{"layer":"thrumpton","text":"The Pigot family built Thrumpton Hall in rose-coloured brick, and it was largely complete by 1617. In the 1660s Gervase Pigot altered and improved it. Its Jacobean cantilevered staircase was carved from the estate's own timber, with John Webb, a pupil of Inigo Jones, overseeing the work.","source":{"title":"Thrumpton Hall